  "articleBody": "It's one thing to have a website, and another to have one that's truly AI-friendly. As answer engines and AI overviews become the norm, your content needs to speak to machines just as much as to people. Many businesses wonder if their existing content is up to snuff or if they need a complete overhaul. Let's break down what AI-friendliness really means for your website.\n\nFirst off, AI loves clarity and directness. Your content should be structured to provide clear, concise answers to specific questions. Think about how someone would ask a question to Siri or Google Assistant; your page should have that answer readily available without a lot of fluff. This means breaking down complex topics into digestible paragraphs and making facts easily extractable. Stuffing keywords or hiding answers behind tabs and accordions that might not render for crawlers can hinder visibility.\n\nNext, factual accuracy and originality are paramount. AI systems are increasingly sophisticated at discerning high-quality, trustworthy information from generic or poorly sourced content. Supporting claims with reliable sources and having clear authorship signals can significantly boost your content's credibility in the eyes of AI. Also, ensure your business and contact information is consistently accurate across your site and other platforms, as AI uses this for entity verification.\n\nFinally, the technical accessibility of your content plays a huge role. While Google says no special AI schema is *required*, well-implemented structured data (like FAQPage or Article schema) helps AI understand the context and purpose of your content. Moreover, ensuring your pages load quickly and are mobile-friendly contributes to a positive page experience, which AI systems consider when determining content quality.\n\n**What to Do Next**\n\nStart by reviewing your most important service or product pages. Can you easily identify direct answers to common customer questions on these pages? Are those answers presented clearly, without being buried in long paragraphs or inaccessible formats? Consider adding or refining relevant structured data, ensuring it accurately mirrors your visible content. If you're unsure, testing your pages with Google's Rich Results Test can give you initial insights into how well your structured data is interpreted.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
}